    "https://jobs.wichita.edu/jobs/senior-investigator-civil-rights-title-ix-wichita-kansas-united-states": {
      "title": "Senior Investigator- Civil Rights & Title IX",
      "description": "Senior Investigator- Civil Rights & Title IX - Wichita, Kansas, United States Senior Investigator- Civil Rights & Title IX - Wichita, Kansas, United States social--blog Kiosk mode Senior Investigator- Civil Rights & Title IX Office of CivilRights,TitleIX & ADA | Staff Senior Investigator- Civil Rights & Title IX 499501 Staff Department: Office of Civil Rights, Title IX & ADA Compliance Campus Location: Wichita, KS - WSU Main Campus Hire Type: Full Time Pay: Sr. range= $62 - 70,000/yr, Standard range= $53 - 60,000/yr (based on qualifications) Work Schedule: General Office Hours; work is performed on-site and in person. Export Compliance Requirement: No export control requirement. Job Story The Role: Objective Investigation & Policy Compliance We are seeking a precise, analytical professional to join our team as a Civil Rights and Title IX Investigator. In this role, you will serve as a neutral fact-finder, managing complex cases and ensuring strict compliance with institutional policies and federal regulations. This position requires a high degree of intellectual multitasking. You must balance objective fact-gathering with a calm, professional demeanor while navigating stressful situations and complex policy frameworks simultaneously. If you have a proven track record conducting full-fledged investigations, civil rights compliance reviews, or discrimination claims, along with the analytical skills detailed above, we want to hear from you. We are actively reviewing candidates for either the Senior Investigator OR Investigator tiers. Final level and compensation will be determined based on your depth of relevant investigative experience. Apply today to be considered for this crucial role on our compliance team. Job Summary Independently conducts investigations into allegations of discrimination, harassment, sexual misconduct, relationship (dating) violence, and stalking involving University community members. Acts as a neutral party and provides an unbiased fact-finding report. Essential Functions • Independently investigates complaints of discrimination and harassment, including but not limited to, reports of protected-class discrimination, sexual misconduct, sexual harassment, sexual violence, and gender-based discrimination. • Composes comprehensive investigative reports that clearly identify the issues investigated, summarizes the evidence found, including information gathered from interviews conducted during the investigatory process, analyzes the matter within a legal framework, applies relevant laws, policies and procedures and makes findings and recommendations. • Collaborates with stakeholders and consults Equal Opportunity and/or Title IX policies to review and investigate complaints while ensuring a prompt, thorough and equitable response to reports and incidents. • Provides periodic, timely updates regarding status of complaints received to help identify and address any patterns or systemic problems and to ensure that applicable policies are communicated to University stakeholders. • Assists in coordinating responses and/or position statements for civil rights charges filed externally, including but not limited to, charges filed with the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, the Kansas Human Rights Commission, and the Office of Civil Rights. • Develops, implements, coordinates and conducts educational programs and initiatives in support of institutional compliance and campus safety. Maintains training records, monitors data integrity and reporting obligations. Job Duties Required Education and Experience: 60 hours of college coursework in social work, criminal justice, public administration, education. Six (6) years of experience in conducting investigations into student and/or employee misconduct related to nondiscrimination, equal access, equal employment opportunity and affirmative action laws, rules and regulations or equivalent experience. Every 30 hours of college coursework beyond 60 hours can be substituted for one (1) year of experience, up to 180 hours. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Collaborates and consults with Equal Opportunity and/or Title IX policies to review and investigate complaints. Conduct investigations individually or as part of a joint team. Advanced interviewing and active listening techniques; Investigates complaints of discrimination, harassment, and retaliation. Professional and legally defensible report writing; Ability to composes comprehensive investigative reports that clearly identify the issues investigated. High emotional intelligence and impartiality; Adept in use of a computer to access, interpret, and record information; Coordinates and collaborates with University departments and compliance partners to support the University. Tactical communication skills to provide advice and counsel in difficult conflicts and disputes; Provides periodic, timely updates regarding status of complaints received to help identify and address any patterns or systemic problems. Develops, implements, coordinates and conducts educational programs and initiatives to educate and support faculty, staff, and students. Additional Information: We will be considering applicants who could meet either the senior level or the standard investigator requirements for the position. Standard Investigator requirements: 60 hours of college coursework in social work, criminal justice, public administration, education. Four (4) years of experience in conducting investigations into student and/or employee misconduct related to nondiscrimination, equal access, equal employment opportunity and affirmative action laws, rules and regulations or equivalent experience. Every 30 hours of college coursework beyond 60 hours can be substituted for one (1) year of experience, up to 120 hours. Senior Level requirements: 60 hours of college coursework in social work, criminal justice, public administration, education. Six (6) years of experience in conducting investigations into student and/or employee misconduct related to nondiscrimination, equal access, equal employment opportunity and affirmative action laws, rules and regulations or equivalent experience. Every 30 hours of college coursework beyond 60 hours can be substituted for one (1) year of experience, up to 180 hours. Physical Requirements: Ability to remain in a stationary position. Frequent 60-90% Ability to operate a computer and other office equipment including but not limited to phone, printers, copiers, and calculators. Frequent 60-90% Ability to communicate with others and accurately exchange information. Frequent 60-90% Ability to interpret effectively, accurately and impartially, both receptively and expressively. Frequent 60-90% Additional Physical Requirement: Apply Now First Name (required) d24e2a11 Last Name (required) beb49d8c Email (required) 7b97eaf8 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at Wichita State University. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). Privacy Policy Thank you Sign Up for Job Alerts! First Name (required) 9c2f1c24 Last Name (required) 979c099d Email (required) 61ffe41e Job Alert 64221516 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Staff Staff Department(s) Office of CivilRights,TitleIX & ADA Office of CivilRights,TitleIX & ADA Send Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at Wichita State University. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency