    "https://jobs.wichita.edu/jobs/temporary-assistant-educator-assistant-teaching-professor-educational-psychology-wichita-kansas-united-states": {
      "title": "Temporary Assistant Educator/Assistant Teaching Professor - Educational Psychology",
      "description": "Temporary Assistant Educator/Assistant Teaching Professor - Educational Psychology - Wichita, Kansas, United States Temporary Assistant Educator/Assistant Teaching Professor - Educational Psychology - Wichita, Kansas, United States social--blog Kiosk mode Temporary Assistant Educator/Assistant Teaching Professor - Educational Psychology College of Education | Faculty Temporary Assistant Educator/Assistant Teaching Professor - Educational Psychology 499811 On Site Faculty Department: Counseling, Educational Leadership, Educational and School Psychology (CLES) Campus Location: Wichita, KS - WSU Main Campus Hire Type: Full Time Pay: $ 32,500 for Spring semester Work Schedule: Monday - Friday - afternoon and evening classes; work is performed on-site and in person. Export Compliance Requirement: No export control requirement. Job Story The Counseling, Educational Leadership, Educational and School Psychology (CLES) department is accepting applications for a temporary, non-tenure track position to provides graduate instruction, student advising, and dissertation support, while contributing to curriculum development, faculty collaboration, and institutional service. This position is needed for the Spring 2027 semester beginning January 4, 2027. There is potential for the appointment to be extended through the 2027/2028 academic year. The position would be hired as an Assistant Educator or Assistant Teaching Professor based on the selected candidate’s education and experience. Job Summary Teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels using various instructional deliveries (e.g., traditional (face-to-face), hybrid, and fully online). To foster applied learning into the course design and engage with industry and local business community applied projects. To advance knowledge through scholarly activities (e.g., active involvement in committees, student mentorship, recruitment and retention initiative, outreach activities, etc.) Essential Functions Meet expectations for teaching and service, as set forth by the department and college. Job Duties • Teach graduate courses in Educational Psychology and doctoral-level research methods. • Advise and mentor graduate students regarding academic progress, research development, and degree completion. • Serve on and/or chair doctoral dissertation committees and provide research-methodology support. • Contribute to curriculum planning, program operations, and student recruitment and retention efforts. • Collaborate with faculty across the Ph.D. in Education and Behavioral Studies. • Participate in appropriate departmental, college, and university service activities. Required Education and Experience: Graduate level degree Teaching experience Faculty Requirements Assistant Educator - graduate degree in Educational Psychology or a closely related field Assistant Teaching Professor - terminal degree in Educational Psychology or a closely related field Please note that the degree must be completed prior to January 2027 Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Evidence of effective teaching or the potential for effective teaching at the graduate level. Ability to teach in multiple instructional formats, including in-person, hybrid, and online courses. Commitment to supporting graduate students from varied academic, professional, and personal backgrounds. Ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, and university partners. Demonstrated expertise in quantitative research methods, with the ability to teach graduate-level quantitative methods courses and serve on dissertation committees involving quantitative research. Preferred Qualifications: Earned doctorate in Educational Psychology or a closely related field. Preference may be given to candidates with graduate preparation or demonstrated expertise in quantitative research methodology, learning and cognition, measurement, program evaluation, or a related area. Experience teaching graduate-level Educational Psychology or research-methods courses. Experience advising or mentoring master’s or doctoral students. Experience serving on dissertation committees or supporting dissertation research. Experience teaching in online or hybrid formats. Evidence of scholarly activity, applied research, or interdisciplinary collaboration. Experience working within a doctoral program or research-intensive university environment Preferred License/Certifications/Training: No professional license or certification is required. Relevant training or certification in quantitative analysis, qualitative research, mixed methods, program evaluation, assessment, or online instruction is preferred. Additional Information: This position is for the Spring 2027 semester with the potential to extend into the 2027-2028 academic year. Physical Requirements: Ability to remain in a stationary position – Frequent 60-90%; Ability to operate a computer and other office equipment including but not limited to phone, printers, copiers, and calculators – Frequent 60-90% Apply Now First Name (required) 3c783542 Last Name (required) bd0ce8e5 Email (required) 3d57d53d Apply Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at Wichita State University. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). Privacy Policy Thank you Sign Up for Job Alerts! First Name (required) 8bb337c7 Last Name (required) 05b6178f Email (required) eba6941d Job Alert 1249fe69 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Faculty Faculty Department(s) College of Education College of Education Send Please wait... Done Not You?